# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""English translations, batch 11 (40 figures)."""
EN = {
"沙得拉": dict(lesson="Even if not, no idol worship—the Lord is present in the furnace",
 summary="Shadrach was one of Daniel's three friends, serving in Babylon. When Nebuchadnezzar set up a golden image commanding all to bow, he with Meshach and Abednego refused, and was thrown into the blazing furnace, yet walked unharmed in the fire, and the king saw a fourth figure 'like a son of the gods.'",
 witness="Facing the blazing furnace, Shadrach declared: our God can deliver us from the furnace, and even if not, we will never serve your idol. This 'even if not' faith is not conditioned on outcome, but grounded only in God being worthy of worship. God was indeed with them in the fire, not a hair singed. The fourth figure in the furnace is traditionally seen as a pre-incarnate appearance of Christ—the Lord with His suffering people.",
 follow=["Holding the bottom line of worship under persecution","Holding an 'even if not' faith, not bargaining with God","Standing together with spiritual companions, strengthening one another"],
 caution=["Compromise in ease often precedes failure before the furnace; be set apart in daily life"],
 applications=["Refuse demands against your faith in the workplace, even at a cost","Practice not making being answered a condition for trusting God","Make spiritual companions who can go into the furnace with you","Meditate on the Lord being with you in the fire when persecuted"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You are the fourth in the furnace, walking with Your suffering servants. Give me an 'even if not' faith, not bowing to idols under any pressure, knowing You are with me in the blazing fire. Amen."),
"米煞": dict(lesson="Rather the furnace than bow to the golden image; worship cannot be compromised",
 summary="Meshach was one of Daniel's three friends, holding high office in Babylon with Shadrach and Abednego. He refused to bow to Nebuchadnezzar's golden image, was thrown into the blazing furnace, kept by God's presence, with not even the smell of fire on him.",
 witness="Meshach, in high office in a foreign land, did not forget whose he was. When the whole nation bowed before the golden image, he chose to stand; when the king's fury and the furnace loomed, he with his companions answered: O king, we have no need to answer you in this matter. His calm steadfastness witnessed that true worship is not for trade, and God responded to his loyalty with His presence in the fire.",
 follow=["Holding loyalty to God even in high office","Not rushing to defend himself before threats, quietly entrusting","Holding true worship in one heart with his companions"],
 caution=["Standing when all bow requires spiritual steadiness built up in daily life"],
 applications=["Practice spiritual standing in a conforming environment","Give God the sovereignty over your reputation and life","Treasure and guard the spiritual friendship of worshipping together","Respond to persecution with quiet steadfastness, not argument"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, strengthen my heart, that where all bow to idols I may worship You alone. However hot the furnace, may I walk in it with You, with no smell of fire on me. Amen."),
"亚伯尼歌": dict(lesson="Bound into the fire yet set free in the fire",
 summary="Abednego was one of Daniel's three friends, who with Shadrach and Meshach refused to bow to the golden image and was thrown bound into the blazing furnace. The ropes binding them were burned off, the three walked in the fire with the fourth figure like a son of the gods, and came out unharmed.",
 witness="Abednego was thrown bound into the furnace, but the fire only burned off the ropes binding him. The world thought the furnace was the end, but God made it the start of walking with the Lord and being promoted by the king's order. His experience witnesses: people can bind God's servant, but cannot block God's presence; what persecution burns is often only our bonds.",
 follow=["Seeing persecution as an occasion to experience God's presence","Trusting God reigns even when bound","Giving glory to God Most High after being delivered"],
 caution=["Never let high office and ease soften your stance of worship"],
 applications=["Look back on the 'furnaces' of your life, counting how God burned off bonds","Intercede for the persecuted body, asking the Lord to walk with them in the fire","Look in trial to the Son of Man who is present","Publicly witness God's works after being set free"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, thank You for walking with Your servants in the blazing fire, burning off the bonds yet preserving life. Make me experience the mystery of this presence in trial, coming out of the furnace with Your praise on my lips. Amen."),
"伯沙撒": dict(lesson="One who profanes holy things and does not fear God is weighed and found wanting",
 summary="Belshazzar was the last king of Babylon, who at a great feast drank from the gold and silver vessels plundered from the Jerusalem temple, praising false gods. A hand suddenly wrote on the wall, and Daniel explained: God has numbered the days of his kingdom; he is weighed in the balances and found wanting. That night Belshazzar was killed, and the kingdom went to the Medes.",
 witness="Belshazzar knew the lesson of his predecessor Nebuchadnezzar being humbled for pride, yet was haughty, even reveling and profaning with the temple vessels. His witness is negative: a person can fall from the high platform of revelry into destruction in one night, for the Judge's finger had already written on the wall. He warns the world—whoever profanes the holy must be judged; only the fear of God is the beginning of wisdom.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Not humbling himself despite knowing his predecessor's lesson, sin upon sin","Profaning holy vessels, treating the holy as entertainment, invites swift judgment","Security in revelry is false; God's balance weighs at any moment"],
 applications=["Treat holy things and God's name with reverence","Humble yourself and repent in time, learning from history and others' failures","Don't be numbed by revelry and success; constantly examine your life's deficits","While it is still today, turn to the God who holds your breath"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, save me from Belshazzar-like arrogance and profanity, that I may fear Your name and treasure Your holy things all my life. When I am weighed in the balance, may I, by Your righteousness, not be found wanting. Amen."),
"歌篾": dict(lesson="The covenant-breaking unfaithful are redeemed at great price by covenant-keeping love",
 summary="Gomer was the prostitute the prophet Hosea married at God's command, who after marriage still played the harlot and left, falling to the point of being sold. God commanded Hosea to love her again, redeeming her back with silver and barley, symbolizing God's undying love for unfaithful Israel.",
 witness="Gomer's story is a mirror of the human heart: loved yet covenant-breaking, freed yet enslaved. But the center of the story is not her unfaithfulness, but that unrelenting love—Hosea bought her back in the marketplace, just as God redeemed His covenant-breaking people at great price. Her redemption witnesses: no fall is too deep for Christ's redemption to reach.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Adultery in the heart makes one forsake living water for broken cisterns","Covenant-breaking after grace is sadder than never knowing grace","The end of sin is being sold as a slave, losing freedom and dignity"],
 applications=["Examine whether there is a 'lover' (idol) in your heart taking God's place","Don't despair in your fall; turn back and receive the Lord's redemption","After being redeemed, live with a grateful, covenant-keeping heart","Love the hard-to-love with God's unrelenting love toward you"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, I once, like Gomer, broke covenant and wandered far, yet You redeemed me at the great price of the cross. Heal the unfaithfulness in my heart, that from now I may belong to You alone, resting in Your never-letting-go love. Amen."),
"约拿": dict(lesson="Three days in the fish foreshadow Christ; God's mercy crosses human narrowness",
 summary="Jonah was sent to proclaim judgment to Nineveh, but fled to Tarshish, was swallowed by a great fish three days and nights in the sea, and after a prayer of deliverance obeyed and went. All Nineveh repented, but Jonah was angry at God's mercy, and God used a plant to teach him to feel a heart of mercy.",
 witness="In the deep of the fish's belly Jonah prayed 'salvation belongs to the LORD,' and experienced rescue, spat back from the place of death. Jesus Himself named it: as Jonah was three days and nights in the belly of the great fish, so the Son of Man—he became the most direct foreshadowing of the Lord's death and resurrection. At the same time his narrowness and God's mercy contrast, reminding us God loves sinners far beyond our hearts.",
 follow=["Still praying toward God's temple in the deep","Receiving a second commission after failure, obeying anew","Preaching God's word even if only one brief sentence"],
 caution=["Fleeing the call drags self and others into the storm","Hatred of enemies can be narrower than God's judgment","Angry over a plant—angry over small things yet not caring for souls"],
 applications=["Obey the call you keep fleeing","Pray for the people you least want to bless","Recite in the depths 'salvation belongs to the LORD'","Meditate on the Lord's power three days in the earth and risen","Ask God to put His mercy for the lost into your heart"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You said the sign of Jonah points to Your death and resurrection. Forgive my rebellion in fleeing the call and my narrow heart, that I may turn back from the fish-belly deep and go to this generation's Nineveh with Your mercy. Amen."),
"大利乌": dict(lesson="God rules the hearts of kings to accomplish the restoration of His temple",
 summary="Darius was a Persian king who, when the temple rebuilding was questioned, ordered a search of the archives, found Cyrus' decree, confirmed and funded the rebuilding command, forbade anyone to hinder it, and the temple was completed.",
 witness="When enemies tried to use the government to stop the temple work, Darius' search and decree became the very safeguard of God's work—not only permitting the rebuilding, but supplying the cost from the king's treasury. He witnesses Proverbs' word that the king's heart is in the LORD's hand: God can turn even Gentile power to serve the restoration of His dwelling, and no power can truly hinder His work.",
 follow=["Investigating the facts justly, not heeding accusation one-sidedly","Firmly supporting the good work once confirmed, to the end"],
 caution=["Authority not submitted to God's will is finally vanity"],
 applications=["Pray for those in power, that God use them for good works","When hindered, trust God can turn the situation around","Support God's kingdom work within your sphere of authority","Find out the truth before deciding, not lightly believing slander"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, the hearts of kings are in Your hand. Thank You for preserving the temple's completion through Darius' decree. Rule today's authorities too, that the building of Your church go unhindered and Your name be glorified. Amen."),
"亚达薛西": dict(lesson="God makes a Gentile king pave the way for His city and law's restoration",
 summary="Artaxerxes was a Persian king who first permitted Ezra to bring the law back to teach the people, then sent the cupbearer Nehemiah back to Jerusalem to rebuild the wall, granting a decree and materials, becoming a key driver of the return and rebuilding.",
 witness="Ezra said: blessed be the LORD, the God of our fathers, who put such a thing as this into the king's heart, to beautify the house of the LORD in Jerusalem. Nehemiah found favor before the king because the good hand of God was on him. Artaxerxes' decree witnesses: the pen of history is in God's hand, and even an empire's administrative orders serve the restoration of God's city and law.",
 follow=["Treating subordinates well and granting their proper spiritual requests","Supporting good work with real resources"],
 caution=["Once heeded slander and stopped the work (Ezra 4); those in power should examine accusations carefully"],
 applications=["Trust God can accomplish His plan through unbelieving rulers","Like Nehemiah, bring your request to God first, then to people","Open doors and supply resources for those with a mission in your post","Pray for national leaders to be led by God"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You moved Artaxerxes to grant the rebuilding of Your city. Move the hearts of those in power today too, paving the way for the gospel and the building of the church; may the authority of all nations be under Your feet. Amen."),
"以利亚实": dict(lesson="A priest allied with the world defiles God's house",
 summary="Eliashib was the high priest in Nehemiah's time, who had taken part in building the Sheep Gate, yet became allied by marriage with the enemy Tobiah, even preparing for him a large room in the temple courts to store furniture. When Nehemiah returned he threw out all Tobiah's furniture and cleansed the room.",
 witness="Eliashib's failure is a heavy warning bell: as high priest he should have guarded the temple's holiness, yet for personal ties and in-law relations he welcomed God's enemy into God's courts. When holy office allies with worldly power, God's house is breached first from within. He stands in contrast to Christ—the High Priest who cleansed the temple, without defilement.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["The higher the spiritual office, the greater the harm of alliance with the world","Sentiment and in-law ties can be a breach for compromising truth","Diverting a place for holy offerings to private use is contempt of God"],
 applications=["Guard the church's holiness, not letting interest relations invade service","Examine whether you compromise truth for the sake of sentiment","Support church leaders in enforcing discipline by truth","Clear the inner room occupied by 'Tobiah's furniture' and give it to the Lord"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You cleansed the temple, and would cleanse this temple that is me. Shine on the corners in me secretly allied with the world, throw out all the defiling 'furniture,' that my service may be holy and without blemish. Amen."),
"多比雅": dict(lesson="The mocker who hinders God's work is finally cleared out of the sanctuary",
 summary="Tobiah was an Ammonite official who with Sanballat mocked, threatened, and schemed to hinder Nehemiah's rebuilding of the wall, infiltrated the upper class of Judah through in-law ties, even occupied a room in the temple, and was finally thrown out together with his furniture by Nehemiah.",
 witness="Tobiah's tactics were endless: first mocking 'even a fox going up would break their stone wall,' then threatening assassination, finally switching to infiltration—correspondence, marriage alliances, lodging in the temple. His failure warns us: the enemy, failing in open attack on God's work, infiltrates secretly. But the wall was still completed, and his furniture was thrown out the door—whoever resists the building of God's dwelling must finally fail.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Mockery is the most common weapon for attacking God's work","The enemy habitually infiltrates God's community through networks of relationships","Worldly power occupying a place in the sanctuary will be cleared out"],
 applications=["When you hear mockery, learn from Nehemiah to pray first then keep working","Beware spiritual infiltration in the name of 'friendly relations'","Guard the set-apart space in church and heart","Don't ally with forces that hinder the gospel"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, keep me and Your church, seeing through every scheme of mockery, threat, and infiltration. Cleanse Your dwelling, that the work of building stand firm and be completed in Your hand. Amen."),
"参巴拉": dict(lesson="Ridicule, armed threat, and conspiracy cannot stop the kingdom's rebuilding",
 summary="Sanballat was a Horonite, a leader of forces around Samaria, who was greatly angered hearing someone sought the good of Israel. He ridiculed the Jews rebuilding the wall, conspired with Tobiah and Geshem to attack Jerusalem, schemed to lure and kill Nehemiah, and hired prophets to threaten him—all in vain.",
 witness="Sanballat represents all forces hostile to the rebuilding of God's kingdom: first using contempt to dissolve morale, then armed threat, then the trap of a 'meeting on the plain' and the threat of false prophets. Nehemiah, working with one hand and holding a weapon with the other, prayed repeatedly and saw through the schemes; the wall was finished in fifty-two days, and the enemies 'were greatly cast down, for they perceived that this work was done by our God.' His utter failure witnesses: those who resist God are put to shame.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Being angered at God's people being blessed is a mindset hostile to God","False-spiritual intimidation (hired prophets) is more deceptive than open attack","An enemy who schemes after each defeat reminds us to fight until the work is done"],
 applications=["Don't stop the work when mocked; give the shame to God","Keep spiritual discernment toward 'friendly invitations'","Work with one hand and hold a weapon with the other: serve while watchful in prayer","Respond to all opposition by glorifying God in the work's completion"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, though the enemy mocked and threatened, the work of Your wall did not stop. Give me Nehemiah-like vigilance and perseverance, seeing through Sanballat-like schemes, until the work You entrust is finished. Amen."),
"基善": dict(lesson="Joining to spread rumors cannot stop God's work",
 summary="Geshem was an Arab leader who with Sanballat and Tobiah mocked Nehemiah, spread the rumor that the Jews rebuilt the wall to rebel against the king, and took part in the plot to lure Nehemiah to a meeting on the plain to harm him—all without success.",
 witness="Geshem (also called Gashmu), an Arab leader, joined Sanballat and Tobiah, first mocking, then threatening, then spreading the rumor 'you want to rebel,' obstructing Nehemiah's rebuilding in every way. Yet the wall was still finished in fifty-two days—human alliance and rumor cannot stop the work God has ordained.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Joining to mock, threaten, and spread rumors to obstruct God's work","Attacking those who do good with public opinion and rumors"],
 applications=["Don't take part in mocking, rumoring, or attacking those who do good","When obstructed, focus on the work, unmoved by rumors","Trust God's work is not hindered by human alliances"],
 prayer="Lord, Geshem joined to spread rumors to obstruct the wall, yet the wall was still built. Make me not take part in mockery and rumor, and when obstructed focus on the work, firmly trusting no one can stop Your work. Amen."),
"哈曼": dict(lesson="Pride and hatred make one die on the gallows he built himself",
 summary="Haman was an Agagite, prime minister of the Persian Empire, who, enraged that Mordecai would not bow to him, plotted to exterminate all the Jews in the kingdom in one day, and built a gallows fifty cubits high for Mordecai. Esther held a banquet to expose his plot, and Haman was hanged on the gallows he had prepared for Mordecai.",
 witness="Haman reached the height of office, yet had all his joy taken by one Jew who would not bow—pride is never satisfied. He cast Pur to exterminate God's people, the very picture of the satanic accuser and destroyer. Yet God turned everything behind the scenes: the king, sleepless, read of Mordecai's deed; Haman was forced to lead his enemy's horse in procession, and finally died on the gallows he prepared. The enemy ruined by his own scheme foreshadows Satan's self-destruction at the cross.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Pride makes all honor unable to feed a hateful heart","The pit dug and the gallows built for others become one's own execution ground","To oppose God's people is to oppose God Himself"],
 applications=["Examine the resentment in your heart toward one person or matter, and deal with it early","Don't exalt yourself by scheming, lest you reap it yourself","When mistreated, quietly wait for God's behind-the-scenes reversal","Remember the cross: the enemy's 'victory' is the place of its defeat"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, on the cross You turned the accuser's scheme into its own defeat. Cleanse the root of pride and bitterness from my heart, keep me from digging pits and building gallows for others, trusting only Your hand that rules behind the scenes. Amen."),
"约书亚大祭司": dict(lesson="The filthy garments removed, the fine garments freely put on",
 summary="Joshua was the high priest after the return from exile, who with Zerubbabel led the rebuilding work. In Zechariah's vision he stood in filthy garments before the Angel of the LORD, with Satan accusing at his side; God rebuked Satan, ordered the filthy garments removed, clothed him in rich robes, and foretold 'My servant the Branch' would come.",
 witness="Joshua stood in the heavenly court, speechless—he and the people he represented were indeed filthy. But the wonder of salvation is just here: not that he cleansed himself, but that God said 'I have taken your iniquity away from you, and I will clothe you with rich garments.' Satan's accusation was rebuked, a brand plucked from the fire was preserved. His vision directly introduces the promise of the 'Branch,' pointing to Christ the High Priest who truly removes the land's sin.",
 follow=["Frankly admitting filthiness, standing before God to receive cleansing","After being clothed anew, faithfully walking in God's ways and keeping the office","Bearing the rebuilding mission in one heart with his companion (Zerubbabel)"],
 caution=["After being cleansed, if one does not walk in the Lord's ways, the promise of office is shaken"],
 applications=["When pressed by accusation, declare the Lord has rebuked Satan","Not by self-cleansing but the Lord's change of clothes: stand daily by grace","After receiving forgiveness, seriously keep a holy service","Look to the Branch who 'removes iniquity in a single day'"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You are the High Priest and Branch who removes our iniquity. Thank You for rebuking the accuser, removing my filthy garments, and clothing me with Your robe of righteousness. Make me faithfully walk in Your ways, serving to the end. Amen."),
"夏甲": dict(lesson="Where one is rejected by people is exactly where God watches over",
 summary="Hagar was Sarai's Egyptian maid, given to Abram as a concubine because of Sarai's barrenness, who after conceiving was mistreated and fled to the wilderness. The Angel of the LORD found her in the wilderness and gave a promise; she called God 'the God who sees.' Later she and Ishmael were driven out, and God again heard the child's voice in the wilderness and saved them.",
 witness="Hagar is the first person in Scripture to whom 'the Angel of the LORD' appeared—an oppressed, fleeing foreign slave woman. In the wilderness at the end of her road she met the God who actively sought her, and named God 'You are the God who sees me.' Her witness shows: God's eye does not favor the lowly less; the place people reject is the place God appears.",
 follow=["Believing God sees and hears amid rejection and mistreatment","Like Hagar, pouring out her heart to God in the wilderness, obeying His guidance","Remembering and praising 'the God who sees,' witnessing to His care"],
 caution=["Hagar despised her mistress Sarai after conceiving; don't grow proud and contemptuous when shown grace","She appears in the plan of 'fulfilling the promise by the flesh,' reminding us not to replace waiting on God with human methods"],
 applications=["When you feel rejected by people, cry to the Lord who sought Hagar in the wilderness","Intercede for and help the oppressed and wandering near you","Give God a 'name' in hardship—concretely count how He watched over you","Stay humble after grace, not despising those who once wronged you"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You are the One who sought Hagar in the wilderness. When I am rejected and at the end of my road, let me see Your watching eye and hear Your voice of promise. Heal the wound of being mistreated in my heart, remove pride after grace, and let me praise You all my life as the God who sees. Amen."),
"拉麦（该隐后裔）": dict(lesson="The culture of repaying violence with violence is finally replaced by Christ's kingdom of forgiveness",
 summary="Lamech was a descendant of Cain, the first polygamist recorded in Scripture. He boasted to his two wives of killing a man in revenge, declaring 'if Cain is avenged sevenfold, then Lamech seventy-sevenfold,' treating violence and revenge as glory.",
 witness="Lamech has no witness of trusting God; he is an amplification of Cain's way: Cain still feared after killing, but Lamech made a song to boast after killing. His 'seventy-sevenfold revenge' sharply contrasts with Jesus' teaching Peter to forgive 'seventy times seven,' showing that only Christ can stop humanity's cycle of blood for blood.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Beware the mindset that treats revenge as power and violence as glory","Unrestrained sin amplifies generation by generation; flee a culture that boasts in sin","Guard against using talent and achievement (Lamech's line had many craftsmen) to mask forsaking God"],
 applications=["When offended, choose forgiveness, not rehearsing revenge in your heart","Refuse to forward or echo speech that delights in shaming and attacking","Examine your relationships by Christ's 'seventy times seven' standard of forgiveness","Pray for those you have grievances with, asking the Lord to break the cycle of revenge"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Lamech boasted of revenge, but You on the cross said 'Father, forgive them.' Save me from the nature of an eye for an eye, put Your seventy-times-seven forgiveness in my heart, that I be a child of peace in a violent age. Amen."),
"宁录": dict(lesson="An empire that makes a name for itself must fall; only Christ's kingdom endures forever",
 summary="Nimrod was a descendant of Ham through Cush, 'the first mighty one on earth,' 'a mighty hunter before the LORD.' He built the earliest human empire, his kingdom beginning with Babel and other cities, linked to the system of self-glory the Tower of Babel represents.",
 witness="Nimrod has no witness of trusting God; he represents humanity's first concentrated wave of power and ambition after the flood: founding a kingdom by force, making a name by building. Babel's slogan 'let us make a name for ourselves, lest we be scattered' is the spirit of this system. God confused their language to stop it, witnessing that every work exalting self cannot stand before God.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Beware building 'a name for yourself' with talent and achievement, rather than glorifying God's name","Guard against the desire for power and expansion swallowing the fear of God","Don't join collective frenzy resisting God's will by force of numbers"],
 applications=["Examine for whose name the enterprise you're building is","In moments of success deliberately give God glory, confessing all is His gift","Refuse to achieve goals by manipulation and force; learn Christ's gentle service","Pray for those in power, asking God to restrain the pride of human power"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Nimrod made a name and built a kingdom for himself, but You emptied Yourself and took the form of a servant. Save me from the ambition of spreading my own name; let what I build all my life be not a Babel but Your kingdom, that Your name be hallowed. Amen."),
"以实玛利": dict(lesson="What is born of the flesh cannot inherit the promise; the one born by promise is the heir",
 summary="Ishmael was the son of Abraham and the maid Hagar by human means. He mocked Isaac, and was driven out with his mother; Paul in Galatians uses him to represent 'born of the flesh,' over against Isaac born by promise. God still cared for him for Abraham's sake, making him a great nation.",
 witness="Ishmael himself was also heard by God (his name means 'God hears'); in the wilderness when dying God opened Hagar's eyes to see a well. But in salvation history he represents the result of using fleshly methods to 'help' God fulfill the promise: not only failing to accomplish it but bringing lasting tension in the home. His existence witnesses that the son of promise must come from God's power, not human flesh.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Beware substituting a fleshly shortcut for waiting on God's promise","Don't, like Ishmael, mock the son of promise or despise God's chosen","A fleshly beginning brings lasting strife and bondage"],
 applications=["When God's promise is delayed, choose to wait, not make your own 'Ishmael'","Examine which parts of your life are from fleshly effort and which from obedient faith","Ask by Galatians 4: am I a child by promise, or a slave by works?","Respect and treasure the work of promise God established in the church, not despise or mock"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You are the true offspring of God's promise, yet I often try to accomplish spiritual things by the flesh. Forgive my impatience and self-will, teach me to wait quietly for the Father's time, that I be a free child born by promise, no longer under the yoke of slavery. Amen."),
"可拉": dict(lesson="The office of mediator is established by God; one must not seize it",
 summary="Korah was a descendant of Levi through Kohath, already serving at the tabernacle, yet joined Dathan, Abiram, and 250 leaders to attack Moses and Aaron, seeking the priesthood for himself. The earth opened and swallowed the rebels, fire consumed the 250 who offered incense, and God thus made clear His chosen mediator.",
 witness="Korah has no witness of faith, only a warning of usurpation: under the banner of 'all the congregation are holy,' he was in fact discontent with God's arrangement and coveted a higher office. Jude lists him with Cain and Balaam on the way of destruction. Notably, Korah's descendants did not perish, but later wrote several psalms of the sons of Korah, witnessing God's mercy surpassing the fathers' rebellion.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Beware packaging personal ambition with spiritual slogans","Discontent with the service-place God measured to you is the start of rebellion","Inciting a group against the authority God established harms more than yourself"],
 applications=["Be grateful for your service post, not comparing the rank of offices with others","When you have an opinion, communicate appropriately, not inciting, recruiting, and forming factions","Examine your service motive: serving God, or exalting yourself through service?","Draw near to God only through Christ the God-appointed Mediator, not relying on seniority and zeal"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, You are the one Mediator God established, yet I often covet position in my heart and resent my place. Tear down Korah's ambition in me, give me a heart content to be lowly, that I may be faithful to the end in the place You measured to me. Amen."),
"大坍与亚比兰": dict(lesson="To rebel against the leadership God established is to rebel against God Himself",
 summary="Dathan and Abiram were sons of Eliab of the tribe of Reuben, who rebelled with Korah. They refused the summons to come up, instead accusing Moses of bringing the people out of 'a land flowing with milk and honey'—Egypt—and making himself king. The earth opened and swallowed them and their households, a warning for Israel through the generations.",
 witness="The two have no witness of faith; their words expose the nature of rebellion: calling enslaving Egypt 'a land flowing with milk and honey,' and God's salvation Moses' private ambition. This is an inverted accusation against redemption itself. They stood at their tent doors defiant to the end, reminding us: hardening one's heart and refusing God's call ends in sudden judgment.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Beware glamorizing 'Egypt'—calling the old life of slavery good","Shifting discontent with God's arrangement into accusation of leaders","Stubbornness to the end, refusing every call to restoration, brings self-destruction"],
 applications=["In the wilderness-like lows, don't grumble against the Lord who saved you; count the grace of the road of salvation","When you disagree with church leadership, keep a humble, teachable heart, not spreading complaints","While it is still today, respond to God's call to restoration; don't harden your heart","Stay away from the tents of those who form factions, lest you share the guilt of the rebellious"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Dathan and Abiram rebelled against the mediator God established, but You were obedient unto death. Remove the complaining and stubborn heart in me, that I not long for Egypt nor accuse grace, but obey the Father's will all my life, following You into the promise. Amen."),
"亚甲": dict(lesson="To spare God's enemy is to discount God's command",
 summary="Agag was king of the Amalekites. God commanded Saul to destroy the Amalekites completely, but Saul spared Agag and the best of the livestock, disobeying God; Samuel killed Agag before the LORD at Gilgal. For this sparing Saul was rejected by God and lost the throne.",
 witness="Agag has no witness of faith; he represents God's enemy: Amalek had attacked the weary and weak Israelites in the wilderness, and God declared war against them through the generations, to blot out their name (Exod 17; Deut 25:17-19). Agag's preservation exposed the failure of Saul, the people's choice of king, witnessing that only God's chosen true King can fully execute God's justice.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["'Partial obedience' to God's command is rebellion, with consequences—Haman was an Agagite descendant","Don't leave room for sin under the excuse of 'the best of it'","Beware the mindset that, like Amalek, attacks the weak"],
 applications=["Deal with sin thoroughly, leaving no survivor for the desire that 'it's a pity to waste'","Obey God's command to the end, not discounting or cleverly editing it","Protect the weary and weak members in the community, not letting them be attacked","Bring the long-entrenched 'Agag' in your heart by name before the Lord for execution"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Saul lost the throne for sparing Agag, but You fully overcame sin and Satan without compromise. Shine on the 'Agag' preserved in my heart, give me a resolve to break with sin, that I may fully obey You, until Your enemies are made Your footstool. Amen."),
"拿伯": dict(lesson="Rather give up life than sell the inheritance God gave",
 summary="Naboth was a Jezreelite who owned a vineyard right beside King Ahab's palace. Ahab wanted to buy it with money or a trade, but Naboth refused because it was the inheritance the LORD gave his fathers. Jezebel then framed him with false witnesses of blaspheming God and king, and Naboth was stoned to death; God through Elijah declared judgment on Ahab's house.",
 witness="Naboth's witness lies in loyalty to God's law: by the law, the inheritance was apportioned by God and not to be sold permanently (Lev 25:23). Facing royal power and gain, he chose to fear God over the king, and was framed by false witness and unjustly killed outside the city. God was not silent—Elijah was at once sent to pronounce judgment, and dogs licked Ahab's blood where Naboth's blood had flowed. God remembers the blood of the innocent.",
 follow=["Holding to the inheritance and principle God gave, not selling out for power or gain","Fearing God more than those in power","Quietly entrusting amid injustice, trusting God will avenge"],
 caution=["His fate reminds us: faithfulness may bring loss or even the cost of life; count the cost first"],
 applications=["When facing an exchange of gain, first ask whether it touches the bottom line God gave you","Don't take part in or tolerate false witness and the framing of others","Intercede for those wronged today for holding their faith","When wronged, learn from Naboth's Lord—reviled, He did not revile back, entrusting Himself to the just Judge"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Naboth was killed by false witness for holding the inheritance God gave; You even more were condemned and crucified outside the city by false witness for our sake. Give me a loyal heart, rather to suffer loss than sell what You entrust; in injustice give me quietness, trusting You are the avenging Lord. Amen."),
"米甲": dict(lesson="Love that loses the fear of God becomes contempt",
 summary="Saul's daughter, David's wife, who once risked her life to save David, yet later despised him in her heart for dancing exuberantly before the ark.",
 witness="Michal at first truly loved David, even defying her father the king and saving his life by a ruse. But years, the trauma of a marriage taken and given away, and her regard for royal dignity slowly cooled her heart. When David worshipped in abandon before the ark, she looked from the window 'and despised him in her heart.' She represents a danger: former love can degenerate into cold scorn of spiritual fervor. She finally had no child, estranged from her husband in the matter of worship.",
 follow=["Once protecting God's anointed with courage and wit"],
 caution=["Letting trauma and dignity replace her first love","Despising another's simple, fervent worship","Measuring spiritual things by worldly dignity"],
 applications=["Guard your first love, not letting years and hurt cool your heart","Don't mock another's simple fervor for God","Worship before God without regard to people's eyes","Deal with the bitterness in your heart, lest it poison your closest relationship"],
 prayer="Lord, keep my heart's first love for You, not cooled by hurt and worldliness; when others worship You fervently, let me rejoice with them, not stand behind the window despising. Amen."),
"暗嫩": dict(lesson="Indulged lust finally devours itself",
 summary="David's firstborn, who out of lust violated his half-sister Tamar, and two years later was killed by Absalom's design.",
 witness="Amnon's story is a specimen of lust's full trajectory: first an infatuation that 'made him sick,' then satisfying lust by trickery and force, and once the desire was sated, 'he hated her with very great hatred, greater than the love he had for her.' He treated a person as an object of self-satisfaction, then discarded her like trash. Sin unaddressed bred two years of hatred, finally costing his life at a feast. Indulgence brings no satisfaction, only deeper emptiness and destruction.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Treating infatuation as love—in fact self-centered possession","Satisfying lust by trickery and force","Turning from 'love' to hatred once sated, showing there was never true love","Not repenting after sin, letting consequences ferment"],
 applications=["See through the 'sick with longing' infatuation; it is often lust, not love","When temptation comes, flee at once, giving it no chance to set a trap","Respect others as people made in God's image, not tools for self-satisfaction","After wrongdoing, confess and deal with it at once, not letting sin sow a greater disaster"],
 prayer="Lord, Amnon indulged his lust, ruining Tamar and himself. Cleanse my thoughts and intentions, make me respect people and restrain myself, choosing to flee rather than scheme before temptation. Amen."),
"耶西": dict(lesson="What God sees is not what man sees",
 summary="A man of Bethlehem, descendant of Boaz and Ruth, father of David, a key link in the Messianic line.",
 witness="When Samuel came to anoint a king, Jesse passed his seven tall, strong sons by one by one, but left out the youngest, David, tending sheep in the field. God said: do not look on his appearance... for the LORD sees not as man sees; man looks on the outward appearance, but the LORD looks on the heart. Jesse symbolizes 'the overlooked place'—God's choice often falls on the one people despise and omit. This unremarkable Bethlehem family later became the house of David, the house of the Messiah.",
 follow=["Raising David, the man after God's heart","Humble and practical, holding to his livelihood in Bethlehem","Becoming a link of grace in the Messianic line"],
 caution=["Nearly measured God's choice by appearance and birth order, overlooking his youngest son"],
 applications=["Don't judge those around you by appearance, achievement, or birth order","Value and nurture those others overlook","Trust God can raise His work from the most unremarkable place","Be faithful in ordinary duty; God's plan is often hidden in it"],
 prayer="Lord, You raised David from Jesse's overlooked youngest son, and from this house brought the Savior. Give me Your way of seeing people, not by appearance, treasuring those You choose. Amen."),
"大利拉": dict(lesson="Selling a relationship for gain ends in mutual loss",
 summary="A woman bought off by the Philistines, who by tenderness repeatedly probed the secret of Samson's strength, finally getting him captured.",
 witness="Delilah turned an intimate relationship into a transaction. The Philistine lords promised her silver, and day after day she pestered Samson: 'How can you say you love me, when your heart is not with me?' She used the language of love to do the work of betrayal, until Samson was 'vexed to death' and told the truth. She represents one who treats trust as a bargaining chip, selling out the closest for gain. Ironically, Samson's failure was also his own responsibility—he repeatedly gave his heart to those he should not.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Betrayal in fact, in the name of love","Sacrificing another's safety for silver","Exploiting the other's trust and weakness to strike"],
 applications=["Examine relationships that keep probing your weakness and bottom line","Don't give up what you shouldn't because of emotional pestering","Run relationships with honesty, not treating trust as a chip","Keep spiritual vigilance even in intimate relationships"],
 prayer="Lord, Delilah sold out the one who trusted her for silver. Make me treat people with honesty and loyalty, not selling out relationships for gain; and give me vigilance, not giving my heart to what would harm me. Amen."),
"亚希米勒": dict(lesson="Treating God's servant well may cost a price",
 summary="The priest of Nob who, when David was fleeing, gave him the showbread and Goliath's sword, and was later killed by Doeg in Saul's misplaced fury, with all the city's priests slaughtered together.",
 witness="Ahimelech did a good thing—receiving and supplying a fallen, God-anointed man. He did not know he was caught in royal suspicion. Saul, believing Doeg's report, without allowing a defense, killed all eighty-five priests of Nob and the whole city. Ahimelech's fate reminds us: doing good can sometimes bring unreasonable disaster; but his son Abiathar fled to David, and the priesthood of God continued. God does not forget the good deeds of the righteous.",
 follow=["Generously receiving and supplying God's fallen servant","Open and honest, pleading his case with a clear conscience"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Receive the fallen and needy with mercy","Have wisdom in doing good, knowing the possible risk","When wronged, plead frankly and leave the outcome to God","Remember the impact of good deeds extends to the next generation"],
 prayer="Lord, Ahimelech paid with his life for treating Your servant well. Give me a heart of mercy to receive people, not shrinking back even when doing good carries risk, trusting You will remember and preserve the offspring of the righteous. Amen."),
"多益": dict(lesson="A tongue that flatters power can kill",
 summary="An Edomite, Saul's chief herdsman, who reported the priests of Nob receiving David, and personally carried out Saul's slaughter order.",
 witness="When Saul's servants would not lift a hand to kill the LORD's priests, Doeg unhesitatingly did it, killing eighty-five and the whole city. He is the type who flatters power, exchanging others' blood for his own position. Psalm 52 was written about him: 'Why do you boast of evil, O mighty man?... your tongue plots destruction, like a sharp razor.' He shows a truth: how terrible is one willing to do evil to please those in power.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Informing and framing others to please power","Rushing to act when everyone else refuses evil","Trading others' suffering for his own promotion"],
 applications=["Don't be an informer who flatters power and sells out others","When everyone does evil, have courage not to take part","Guard your tongue; it can build up and it can kill","Remember God will require the blood of the innocent"],
 prayer="Lord, Doeg did evil for power with tongue and sword. Cleanse my mouth, that I not inform and frame or harm people to please others; give me courage not to go along when everyone does evil. Amen."),
"瓦实提": dict(lesson="Before the humiliation of power, dignity too has a price",
 summary="Queen of the Persian king Ahasuerus, who, when the king was merry with wine, refused to come out to display her beauty to the drunken officials, and was therefore deposed.",
 witness="Vashti was summoned to come out and 'show her beauty' at the end of a seven-day feast, when all were drunk. She refused. Scripture doesn't state her motive, but she held the line of not being displayed as a plaything. For this she lost the throne. Her exit prepared the way for Esther's entrance—God's sovereignty works above human decisions and failures. Vashti reminds us: holding dignity sometimes has a price, and God can make one person's exit the start of another's story of salvation.",
 follow=["Holding her dignity as a person when made into a plaything to be displayed"],
 caution=["Before tyrannical power, an individual's stance is often ruthlessly crushed"],
 applications=["When objectified and humiliated, hold your dignity as a person","Understand that holding the line sometimes costs","Trust God's sovereignty works above the changes of affairs and even loss of position","Pray for those under the oppression of power"],
 prayer="Lord, Vashti held her dignity before humiliation, and prepared the way for Esther's rise. Give me dignity as a person even when slighted, trusting Your sovereignty works above all changes. Amen."),
"基大利": dict(lesson="Goodness without vigilance can also invite disaster",
 summary="The governor installed by Babylon after Judah's fall, gentle in rule, yet killed because he did not believe the warning that Ishmael would assassinate him.",
 witness="Gedaliah was a gleam of gentle hope on the ruins of a fallen nation. He urged the remnant to settle, serve Babylon, and have peace. He was kind-hearted, but for that also naive—Johanan warned him that Ishmael, instigated by the Ammonite king, would come to assassinate him, but he did not believe and instead entertained him. As a result he was killed at a feast, and the remaining Judean community scattered and fled. His tragedy reminds us: goodness needs vigilance and discernment, or good intentions can brew greater disaster.",
 follow=["Settling the remnant on the ruins, urging people to live in peace","Gentle in governance, willing to give people a way out"],
 caution=["Taking a clear warning of danger lightly","Replacing necessary discernment and precaution with good intentions"],
 applications=["Treat people with goodness, but also have discernment and vigilance","Take credible warnings seriously, not blindly optimistic","Give people hope amid turmoil, but not naively","Ask God for wisdom that is both good and shrewd"],
 prayer="Lord, Gedaliah was kind but lax in precaution, so his good rule was cut short. Give me wisdom both gentle and vigilant, treating people with goodness yet not naive, taking seriously the warnings I ought to heed. Amen."),
"西拿基立": dict(lesson="Boasting before the living God brings shame on oneself",
 summary="King of Assyria who besieged Jerusalem and through the Rabshakeh insolently insulted the LORD, and in one night had 185,000 of his army struck down by an angel.",
 witness="Sennacherib swept through the nations, thinking the LORD was just one of the nations' gods: 'Which of all the gods of the nations has delivered its land out of my hand?' He treated victory over men as capital to overcome God. Hezekiah spread his insulting letter before the LORD and prayed, and God answered through Isaiah: 'Whom have you mocked and reviled?... the Holy One of Israel.' That night the angel of the LORD went out, and the Assyrian camp lay full of corpses. Pride before the living God is the most dangerous folly.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Breeding arrogance from continual victories, exalting himself above God","Openly blaspheming and despising the living God","Relying on force and intimidation, not knowing there is One who rules above"],
 applications=["Don't ignore God because of a string of successes","When threatened and intimidated, learn from Hezekiah to spread the trouble before God in prayer","Remember human power is nothing before God","Beware the spiritual pride that success brings"],
 prayer="Lord, Sennacherib boasted before the living God and lost his whole army in one night. Keep me far from the pride success brings; before intimidation let me look to You alone, for victory belongs to You, not to human might. Amen."),
"哈薛": dict(lesson="Denying the evil in your heart doesn't mean you won't do it",
 summary="A servant of Aram, whom Elisha tearfully foretold would become king and harm Israel; he smothered Ben-hadad and usurped the throne, indeed becoming a great scourge to Israel.",
 witness="Elisha stared at Hazael until he was ashamed, then wept. Hazael asked: 'What is your servant, but a dog, that he should do this great thing?' He could not imagine he would commit the atrocities of slaughtering cities and ripping open pregnant women. Yet the next day he smothered his master with a wet cloth and seized the throne. The terror of Hazael is this: he sincerely thought he would never become such a man, yet step by step became him. We all need to beware—the evil we deny today may be exactly what we do tomorrow.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Deceiving oneself with 'I would never,' underestimating the evil in the heart","Stopping at nothing for power, murdering his master and usurping the throne","Doing once-unimaginable atrocities after gaining power"],
 applications=["Don't lightly say 'I would never'; beware the evil in your own heart","Especially guard against power amplifying corruption when you hold it","Let God's word and trusted people continually shine on your inner self","Deal with ambition early when it stirs, giving it no room to grow"],
 prayer="Lord, Hazael denied the evil in his heart, yet finally did it. Shine on the hidden corruption in me, that I not deceive myself nor be devoured by power and ambition, but guard my heart by Your grace. Amen."),
"加百列": dict(lesson="Faithfully conveying God's word, neither adding nor subtracting",
 summary="An angel standing in God's presence, who conveyed God's message to Daniel, Zechariah, and Mary, announcing the key moments of the plan of salvation.",
 witness="Gabriel is God's messenger, the one who 'stands in the presence of God.' He announced John the Baptist's birth to Zechariah and Christ's birth to Mary: 'Nothing will be impossible with God.' He faithfully conveyed God's word, neither adding nor subtracting, and with comfort: 'Do not be afraid, Mary, for you have found favor with God.' Gabriel represents all who serve faithfully, obey simply, and convey God's word accurately—greatness is not in how important you are, but in whose message you carry.",
 follow=["Conveying God's message faithfully and accurately, neither adding nor subtracting","Comforting those who receive the message with 'do not be afraid'","Fully obeying God's commission"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Be faithful to the original meaning when preaching God's word, not smuggling in your own bias","Willingly stay behind the scenes in service, letting God's message come first","Comfort the fearful with God's word","Believe 'nothing will be impossible with God'"],
 prayer="Lord, Gabriel faithfully conveyed Your word, bringing comfort and hope. Make me too Your faithful messenger, accurately proclaiming Your truth, comforting the fearful with Your word, firmly trusting nothing is impossible with You. Amen."),
"亚利马太的约瑟": dict(lesson="Standing publicly on the Lord's side at the crucial moment",
 summary="A councilor of the Jewish Sanhedrin, secretly a disciple of Jesus, who after the Lord's death boldly asked for the body and buried it in his own new tomb.",
 witness="Joseph of Arimathea was originally 'a disciple secretly for fear of the Jews.' But at the darkest hour, when the Lord was crucified and the disciples scattered, he instead 'boldly' went to Pilate, asked for Jesus' body, and offered his own new tomb. When the cost of following was highest and most dangerous, this hidden disciple stood up. He reminds us: faith must finally move from secret to open, and God often calls us to bravely declare our stance at the hardest time.",
 follow=["Boldly confessing the Lord at the most dangerous time","Generously offering his best (a new tomb) to bury the Lord","Moving from secret discipleship to open standing"],
 caution=["Long hiding his faith out of fear, slow to confess openly"],
 applications=["Let your faith move from secret to open confession","When others shrink back is exactly when you stand up","Offer your best to the Lord","Count the cost of following the Lord and gladly pay it"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Joseph of Arimathea boldly stood up at Your passion, offering his best to bury You. Make me no longer follow secretly, but openly confess You at the crucial moment, offering You my best. Amen."),
"迦玛列": dict(lesson="In matters not yet clear, leave room for God",
 summary="A highly respected Pharisee and teacher of the law in the Sanhedrin, Paul's teacher; when the council wanted to kill the apostles, he urged caution, to let it run and see whether it was from God.",
 witness="When the council was filled with rage to kill the apostles, Gamaliel stood up and, citing history, urged calm: 'If this plan is of man, it will fail; but if it is of God, you will not be able to overthrow them—you might even be found opposing God.' This is rare prudence and reverence—in matters not yet clear, not rushing to suppress by force, but leaving room for God's work. Though he may not have believed, he protected the early church, and taught the later Paul. Wisdom is sometimes shown in restraint.",
 follow=["Keeping calm and prudent amid public fury","With a God-fearing attitude, not lightly judging God's work","Respecting facts, testing truth by history and time"],
 caution=["Prudence still stopping at watching, not reaching real belief"],
 applications=["Stay humble in matters not clear, not rushing to suppress","Be the voice that calms everyone when feelings run high","Test truth by time and facts, leaving judgment to God","Beware 'opposing God's work' without realizing it"],
 prayer="Lord, Gamaliel left room for Your work with a prudent, reverent heart amid the storm. Give me the same wisdom and humility, not pronouncing rash judgments on matters not yet clear, lest I unwittingly resist You. Amen."),
"亚居拉": dict(lesson="A co-worker serving the Lord through family and profession",
 summary="A Jewish tentmaker who with his wife Priscilla received Paul, co-labored and shared the same trade, set up a church in their home, and nurtured Apollos.",
 witness="Aquila and Priscilla are a model couple serving God through daily life. They shared Paul's trade (tentmaking), his work, and his home, turning their profession into a platform for mission. They risked their lives to help Paul, and hearing Apollos preach in Ephesus, 'explained to him the way of God more accurately.' Their home became a gathering place for the church in different cities. They remind us: serving God need not be full-time or from a pulpit; an ordinary family, profession, and hospitality can carry great kingdom influence.",
 follow=["Using profession and family as a platform for service","Hospitable, risking themselves to help the Lord's servant","Gently and accurately nurturing and building up others"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Offer your profession and family to be vessels of service","Receive needy members and servants with hospitality","Help people understand the truth more, gently, at the right time","Husband and wife in one heart, committing together to God's work"],
 prayer="Lord, the couple Aquila served You and built people up through profession, family, and hospitality. Make my daily life too a vessel for You, carrying Your kingdom's work in the ordinary. Amen."),
"百基拉": dict(lesson="A sister who builds people up in the truth",
 summary="The wife of Aquila, often named together with him and frequently listed first; who with her husband taught the eloquent Apollos, helping him understand the way of God more.",
 witness="Priscilla is a highly respected woman co-worker in the New Testament; the couple is mentioned six times, four of which list her before her husband—very unusual for that time, showing her weight in teaching and service. When the eloquent, Scripture-versed Apollos knew only John's baptism, Priscilla and Aquila 'took him and explained to him the way of God more accurately'—not publicly shaming, but privately, gently, accurately building up. She reminds us: building people up in the truth depends on kindness and solidity, not display.",
 follow=["Solid in the truth, able to build up a spiritual leader","Helping people in a gentle, private way, not shaming them","In one heart with her husband, side by side in service"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Work hard in the truth, so you can help others","When correcting or supplementing people, choose a gentle, private way","Value the behind-the-scenes service of building people up","Serve God together as a family unit"],
 prayer="Lord, Priscilla built up Apollos with solidity and gentleness in the truth. Make me work hard in Your word, and help people grow privately with kindness, not seeking display before people. Amen."),
"非比": dict(lesson="A deaconess quietly serving everyone",
 summary="A deaconess of the church at Cenchreae, solemnly recommended by Paul, 'a helper of many and of myself,' traditionally thought to have carried the letter to the Romans.",
 witness="At the close of Romans Paul solemnly recommends Phoebe: calling her a 'deaconess,' instructing the believers to 'receive her in the Lord in a way worthy of the saints,' and saying she 'has been a helper of many and of myself.' A name often passed over, yet she carried the delivery of Paul's most important letter, and by real action supplied and supported many members. Phoebe represents those who do not stand at the front, but quietly hold up the church by service and hospitality—their names may be obscure, but God remembers each one.",
 follow=["Helping many by real service and supply","Faithfully entrusted, bearing an important mission","Holding up the church by hospitality and support"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Value and thank those who quietly serve in the church","Help needy members by real action","Faithfully finish what you're entrusted with, whether visible or not","Take part in God's work through hospitality and supply"],
 prayer="Lord, Phoebe quietly served everyone, and faithfully carried Your servant's letter. Make me willingly serve in unseen places, holding up members by real help, trusting You will remember. Amen."),
"巴底买": dict(lesson="Persistent crying out; faith saves",
 summary="A blind beggar outside Jericho who, though rebuked by the crowd, cried louder for Jesus' mercy, received sight by faith, and followed the Lord on the way.",
 witness="Bartimaeus sat begging by the road, and hearing it was Jesus of Nazareth, cried out: 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!' The crowd rebuked him to be silent, but he cried out all the more. Jesus stopped and called him. He threw off his cloak (a beggar's livelihood), jumped up, and went to Jesus. 'What do you want Me to do for you?' 'Rabboni, let me recover my sight!' Jesus said: 'Your faith has saved you.' He at once saw and followed Jesus on the way. Persistent, all-in faith is heard by the Lord.",
 follow=["Recognizing Jesus as the Son of David (Messiah), persistently crying out","Crying more urgently despite the crowd's hindrance","Following the Lord at once after being healed"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Keep crying to the Lord amid hindrance and coldness","Tell the Lord your need clearly","To follow the Lord, be willing to throw off old reliance (the cloak)","Respond to grace by following the Lord"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Bartimaeus persisted in crying out despite hindrance, and received sight by faith. Give me the same urgent, persistent faith, throwing off old reliance to come to You, and following You all my life after grace. Amen."),
"睚鲁": dict(lesson="At the end of despair, only believe",
 summary="A synagogue ruler of Capernaum who fell down before Jesus for his dying only daughter; when the girl died, the Lord said to him 'do not fear, only believe,' and raised the girl.",
 witness="Jairus was a synagogue ruler of standing, yet for his dying twelve-year-old only daughter he fell at Jesus' feet pleading. On the way, the woman with the hemorrhage delayed Jesus, and news came from home: 'Your daughter is dead; why trouble the Teacher any further?' At that moment of shattered hope, Jesus said to him: 'Do not fear; only believe!' He chose to keep believing. Jesus went in, took the girl's hand, and said: 'Little girl, I say to you, arise!' She got up at once and walked. When people say 'too late,' the Lord says 'only believe.'",
 follow=["Laying down his status, humbly falling down to plead with the Lord","Seeking the only hope when his daughter was dying","Still choosing to keep believing the Lord amid the bad news"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["At the end of despair, still choose to believe the Lord","Don't let status and dignity keep you from coming to the Lord","When people say 'too late,' remember the Lord says 'only believe'","Urgently bring those you love to the Lord"],
 prayer="Lord Jesus, Jairus heard You say 'do not fear, only believe' amid the news that his daughter had died. Strengthen my faith at the end of my despair, trusting that with You nothing is too late. Amen."),
}
